1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:10,644
The speed of light is 299,792,458 metres per
second. Not approximately. Exactly.

2
00:00:11,133 --> 00:00:16,087
Sunlight takes about eight minutes to
reach us. Everyone knows that.

3
00:00:16,137 --> 00:00:20,282
But the number behind it is
strange. It has no error bar.

4
00:00:20,332 --> 00:00:23,078
No last digit still being argued over.

5
00:00:23,128 --> 00:00:26,463
And every other measurement
ever made has one.

6
00:00:26,967 --> 00:00:29,562
The reason is that we
stopped measuring it.

7
00:00:29,612 --> 00:00:34,297
Today the speed of light is a decision — and
everything else bends around it.

8
00:00:34,800 --> 00:00:38,815
Getting there took three hundred
years, a moon of Jupiter,

9
00:00:38,865 --> 00:00:42,879
a spinning wheel with 720 teeth, a
mountain in California,

10
00:00:42,929 --> 00:00:46,734
and a laser that finally made the
question meaningless.

11
00:00:47,200 --> 00:00:49,730
This is how we learned how fast light is.

12
00:00:52,833 --> 00:00:57,616
For most of history, the honest answer was
that light had no speed at all.

13
00:00:57,666 --> 00:01:01,469
Galileo tried to measure it with two
lanterns on two hills,

14
00:01:01,519 --> 00:01:05,648
and found only that light was faster
than two men with shutters.

15
00:01:06,133 --> 00:01:09,355
The first real answer came from
the wrong problem.

16
00:01:09,405 --> 00:01:14,720
In the 1670s, astronomers in Paris were building
tables for the moons of Jupiter —

17
00:01:14,770 --> 00:01:20,152
because a moon that eclipses on schedule is a
clock that everyone on Earth can see,

18
00:01:20,202 --> 00:01:22,769
and a clock like that solves navigation.

19
00:01:23,267 --> 00:01:29,237
Io, the innermost large moon, goes behind
Jupiter every forty-two and a half hours.

20
00:01:29,287 --> 00:01:31,413
Predictable. Except it wasn't.

21
00:01:31,463 --> 00:01:35,547
The eclipses ran early when Earth
was closest to Jupiter,

22
00:01:35,597 --> 00:01:38,303
and late when Earth was farthest away.

23
00:01:38,800 --> 00:01:45,344
In 1676, the Danish astronomer Ole Rømer said
the obvious thing that nobody wanted

24
00:01:45,394 --> 00:01:49,526
to say: the eclipses are on time.
The light is late.

25
00:01:50,033 --> 00:01:52,765
When Earth is on the far
side of its orbit,

26
00:01:52,815 --> 00:01:57,489
the light from Io has to cross the extra
width of that orbit to reach us.

27
00:01:57,539 --> 00:02:01,371
Rømer estimated that crossing took
about twenty-two minutes.

28
00:02:01,867 --> 00:02:04,433
With the orbit sizes we know today,

29
00:02:04,483 --> 00:02:10,489
twenty-two minutes gives a speed of light of
about 227,000 kilometres per second.

30
00:02:10,539 --> 00:02:13,703
That is about twenty-five
per cent too low.

31
00:02:14,200 --> 00:02:19,833
And it did not matter. Rømer's number was
wrong; Rømer's point was right.

32
00:02:19,883 --> 00:02:21,157
Light takes time.

33
00:02:21,207 --> 00:02:27,463
After 1676, the speed of light was a quantity —
something with a value that could

34
00:02:27,513 --> 00:02:28,942
be measured better.

35
00:02:32,033 --> 00:02:36,750
The next step took another 170 years, and
it happened on the ground.

36
00:02:37,267 --> 00:02:43,257
In 1849, Hippolyte Fizeau sent a beam of light
from a house in Suresnes to a mirror

37
00:02:43,307 --> 00:02:49,007
on Montmartre — a round trip of about
seventeen kilometres — and put a spinning

38
00:02:49,057 --> 00:02:50,899
toothed wheel in its path.

39
00:02:51,400 --> 00:02:56,080
Light goes out through a gap between two
teeth, travels to the mirror,

40
00:02:56,130 --> 00:02:57,094
and comes back.

41
00:02:57,144 --> 00:03:01,824
If the wheel has turned by exactly one
tooth while the light was away,

42
00:03:01,874 --> 00:03:05,946
the returning beam hits metal, and
the observer sees nothing.

43
00:03:06,433 --> 00:03:11,267
Fizeau turned the wheel faster and
faster until the light went out.

44
00:03:11,317 --> 00:03:15,713
Seven hundred and twenty teeth,
twenty-five turns per second,

45
00:03:15,763 --> 00:03:21,763
seventeen kilometres of travel — and the speed
of light fell out of the arithmetic:

46
00:03:21,813 --> 00:03:23,950
313,000 kilometres per second.

47
00:03:24,467 --> 00:03:28,597
About four and a half per cent too
high, measured with a wheel,

48
00:03:28,647 --> 00:03:30,588
a lamp and a mirror on a hill.

49
00:03:31,100 --> 00:03:36,937
Thirteen years later, Léon Foucault replaced
the wheel with a rotating mirror,

50
00:03:36,987 --> 00:03:43,125
which let him do the whole experiment on a bench
instead of across a city — with a

51
00:03:43,175 --> 00:03:46,069
light path of only about twenty metres.

52
00:03:46,119 --> 00:03:49,465
In 1862 he got 298,000
kilometres per second,

53
00:03:49,515 --> 00:03:53,842
within about six tenths of a per
cent of the modern value.

54
00:03:54,300 --> 00:03:58,299
For the first time, the number was
right to within a per cent.

55
00:03:58,349 --> 00:04:00,715
The argument moved to the next digit.

56
00:04:03,800 --> 00:04:09,407
In 1926, Albert Michelson ran the experiment
across the sky of southern California

57
00:04:09,457 --> 00:04:12,235
— from Mount Wilson to Mount San Antonio,

58
00:04:12,285 --> 00:04:17,823
a distance of about thirty-five kilometres,
surveyed to within a few centimetres.

59
00:04:18,333 --> 00:04:23,687
At one end, an eight-sided mirror spun at five
hundred and twenty-eight turns a

60
00:04:23,737 --> 00:04:24,166
second.

61
00:04:24,216 --> 00:04:27,655
Light left one face, crossed the
valley, came back,

62
00:04:27,705 --> 00:04:33,265
and struck the next face — but only if the
mirror had turned exactly one eighth of

63
00:04:33,315 --> 00:04:36,275
a revolution while the light
was in the air.

64
00:04:36,767 --> 00:04:43,705
His answer: 299,796 kilometres per
second, give or take one.

65
00:04:44,200 --> 00:04:49,700
By now the limit was no longer the cleverness of
the experiment. It was the ruler.

66
00:04:49,750 --> 00:04:52,205
To state a speed you need a distance,

67
00:04:52,255 --> 00:04:57,146
and the metre itself was a scratch on a bar
of metal in a vault in Paris.

68
00:05:00,233 --> 00:05:02,497
Speed is distance over time.

69
00:05:02,547 --> 00:05:09,191
By the 1960s, time had become extraordinarily
good: atomic clocks could count the

70
00:05:09,241 --> 00:05:13,571
seconds to eleven digits.
Distance could not keep up.

71
00:05:14,067 --> 00:05:15,423
Then came the laser.

72
00:05:15,473 --> 00:05:21,189
A stabilised laser has a frequency you can count
and a wavelength you can measure.

73
00:05:21,239 --> 00:05:26,393
Multiply them together and you get a speed —
directly, in one measurement.

74
00:05:26,900 --> 00:05:31,995
In 1972, at the National Bureau of
Standards in Boulder,

75
00:05:32,045 --> 00:05:39,254
a team led by Kenneth Evenson did exactly that
with a methane-stabilised laser.

76
00:05:39,304 --> 00:05:43,481
Their result: 299,792,456.2
metres per second,

77
00:05:43,531 --> 00:05:51,107
with an uncertainty of 1.1 metres per second — a
hundred times better than anything

78
00:05:51,157 --> 00:05:52,026
before it.

79
00:05:52,533 --> 00:05:54,234
And that created a problem.

80
00:05:54,284 --> 00:05:59,161
The measurement was now more precise than the
definition of the metre it was

81
00:05:59,211 --> 00:06:00,263
measured against.

82
00:06:00,313 --> 00:06:04,089
The uncertainty in the speed of
light was, almost entirely,

83
00:06:04,139 --> 00:06:05,969
the uncertainty in the ruler.

84
00:06:09,067 --> 00:06:17,148
In 1983, the General Conference on Weights and
Measures took the obvious way out.

85
00:06:17,198 --> 00:06:24,376
It stopped measuring the speed of light and
defined it: exactly 299,792,

86
00:06:24,426 --> 00:06:26,584
458 metres per second.

87
00:06:27,100 --> 00:06:32,950
Then it redefined the metre in
terms of that number.

88
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:42,027
A metre is the distance light travels in one
299-million-792-thousand-458th of a

89
00:06:42,077 --> 00:06:42,821
second.

90
00:06:43,333 --> 00:06:44,931
The trade was deliberate.

91
00:06:44,981 --> 00:06:48,424
We gave up the ability to measure
the speed of light,

92
00:06:48,474 --> 00:06:53,961
and in exchange we got a ruler that is the same
everywhere in the universe, forever.

93
00:06:54,467 --> 00:06:59,610
It is the same move that would be made for the
kilogram thirty-six years later:

94
00:06:59,660 --> 00:07:04,605
replace an object with a constant, and let
the object become just an object.

95
00:07:05,100 --> 00:07:08,494
So when someone asks how we know
the speed of light,

96
00:07:08,544 --> 00:07:10,614
the honest answer has two parts.

97
00:07:10,664 --> 00:07:13,197
We measured it for three hundred years,

98
00:07:13,247 --> 00:07:16,376
from a late moon to a spinning
wheel to a laser.

99
00:07:16,426 --> 00:07:21,344
And then we stopped, because we had learned
enough to make it a definition.

100
00:07:21,833 --> 00:07:24,754
The last digit stopped moving in 1983.
